  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1143 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nHigh pressure will start to pull back to the west this weekend, \nopening the door for a little better forcing to move through, \npromoting slightly higher rain/storm chances. Tennessee will be \nfixed between the ridge to the west and a deepening low off the \nNew England coast that will spin a shortwave through the eastern \nhalf of the CWA. A weak cold front will develop out of ahead of \nthis wave that will bring slightly higher rain/storm chances to \nthe area during the day Saturday. Storm evolution and timing \nremain uncertain as models depict varying solutions. Some \ndeterministic guidance suggests a weakening MCS will ride the \nperiphery of the ridge, clipping our northeast counties. Others \nshow a more unorganized development of storms immediately ahead of\nthe cold front. These details will hopefully be worked out in \nfuture runs, but regardless, the severe threat at this time \nappears low as bulk shear and mid-level lapse rates are on the \nlower side. However there will be plenty of instability and \nmoisture, so a few strong to maybe severe storms could be \npossible. Once the front moves through, we will see a very slight \ndrop in temperatures Sunday, but it's still going to be hot. \nFortunately, northerly winds will filter in drier air, so though \nit will be hot, it won't be nowhere near as humid. For the bulk of\nnext week, we will remain in a stagnant pattern where we'll have \nheat, humidity, and daily low chances for diurnally-driven \nthunderstorms. Highs will remain in the 90s with feel-like \ntemperatures near 100 degrees.",
